6. Database design
Start with these tables:
users

id (PK), name, email, password, role


categories

id (PK), category_name


products

id (PK), name, price, availability, category_id (FK)


orders

id (PK), user_id (FK), total_amount, status, created_at


order_items

id (PK), order_id (FK), product_id (FK), quantity, price


payments

id (PK), order_id (FK), amount, payment_status, transaction_reference


Relationships:
- One user can have many orders.
- One order can have multiple order items.
- One product belongs to one category.
- One order can have a payment record.
Use proper transaction handling when placing orders. Do not store raw card details or trust payment status supplied by the frontend.
